module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.green_floor_is_bounded_by_its_gate_population

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data green_floor_is_bounded_by_its_gate_population: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "green_floor_is_bounded_by_its_gate_population" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**a change alters the MEANING of a core-compiler stage (infer, eval, resolve, body lowering) or of an untyped substrate FORM (an Arrow's required edges on `v2.std.node` Node), claims in UNTOUCHED files that exercise that meaning go false, and the required floor reports GREEN because none of them is in the population it runs** (THE INVALID STATE is a required-floor SUCCESS over a head at which claims that were true at the base are false. The floor executes exactly two populations: the static gate, `v2.workflow.required_floor` `required_gate_prefixes` plus its named-module roster, and the changed-witness sublane, the test fns whose OWN lines the diff edited (`required_floor_disposition_with_changed_selection`). The interface-changed consumers of `v2.workflow.floor_subject_seed` are prepared, not executed. A claim outside the gate, whose file has no diff, that reads the changed meaning through a call chain, is in neither.)",

    "THE HARM: a merge-path green that reads as 'this change breaks nothing' when it says only 'this change breaks nothing in the gate'. The broken claims stay false on main until an unrelated author runs them, which is the deferred, misattributed cost DESIGN section 5 names. It is not a selection-algorithm deficit that a better diff reader closes -- see the replay below -- so reading it as one spends effort on a mechanism that selects nothing.",

    "SPECIMEN, gunbc#12361 (MQ-1 PR-A, declared parameter order on the Arrow). At head e659b86fba the required floor was GREEN (run 36256059426) and the PR was approved and enqueued; only a main conflict dequeued it. vivid-ram-65's base-vs-head run (base bd2c586926b, one `gunbc run --function` per claim over the 17 test files that apply hand-built Arrows through infer/eval) found TEN claims true on main and false on the head, in four modules: `v2.test.claim.body_lowering.arrow_body_form_witness` (1), `v2.test.claim.type_param_binder_frame` (4), `v2.test.execution.infer_product_introduction` (2), `v2.test.execution.data_decl_lowering_grounding` (3). None of the four files had a diff at e659b86fba, and every module name begins `v2.`, so no gate prefix (all spelled `test.claim.*`) admits them. The file list was a grep, so claims outside it are unexamined, not clean. All ten were repaired in the PR before it landed (8e8beabee05).",

    "THE REPLAY THAT RULES OUT SELECTION AS THE CLIMB (quiet-newt-443, adhoc-be476b8f-943; parent ruling from neat-boar-16 withdrew the selection model on it). Two candidate relations were measured over this specimen. (a) REVERSE MODULE-IMPORT CLOSURE of the changed modules: 3,545 modules and 17,034 of 24,434 test fns, 70 percent of the claim corpus and several times the whole floor; one body-lowering module alone (eb5987e38f) closes over 40 percent. That is the corpus-denominated superset DESIGN section 5 forbids as an absorbing fallback. (b) DIRECT READERS OF CHANGED DECLARATIONS, body OR interface: the merge-base..head diff changed 14 existing declarations (`resolve_arrow_node`, `infer_application_formals`, `infer_application_argument_inhabitance`, `eval_bind_arrow_params`, five in `v2.compiler.body_lowering_fold`, two in `v2.std.anonymous_binder`, `v2.std.node` `arrow_signature_edges_conform`, `v2.std.type_binder` `arrow_named_labels_conform`, one floor roster). ZERO of the four failing modules reads any of them: they call entry points (`v2.compiler.infer` `infer`, `assemble_program_from_ingest`) and fixture builders in other modules, none of which changed. Direct-reference selection selects 0 of 10. The comparison was a declaration-text diff over top-level items, not the host DeclarationIndex, and its conclusion does not depend on that grain: the failing claims reach the change only transitively.",

    "DISTINGUISHING FACTS, and the neighbours this is not. [[changed_declaration_signature_consumer_unplanned]] is a declaration whose INTERFACE changed and a reader that no longer TYPECHECKS: decidable at compile grain, reached by the direct reader relation, and repaired by seeding readers into Strict preparation. Here every interface is unchanged, every reader still typechecks, and the defect is a BEHAVIOURAL difference reached through a call chain. Two causes are fused in the specimen and have different ceilings. THE SHAPE HALF: an Arrow is an untyped Node edge-bag, so 'an Arrow carries its signature-order edge' was a convention every hand-built fixture had to know, and fixtures that did not know it produced wrong answers rather than refusals. THE BEHAVIOUR HALF: infer and eval themselves now answer differently for some inputs, which no construction can prevent because it is the change's intent.",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: below 1 on the merge path for both halves. The claims were real, discriminating, and red when run; the required path did not run them, and detection was a manual base-vs-head sweep by the change's author. CEILINGS. Shape half: 3, structurally guaranteed -- whether a Node is a well-formed Arrow is decidable, so a construction path that refuses a signature without its order edge makes the fixture defect a refusal at build time rather than a claim some floor must select. Behaviour half: 2, mechanically preventable, and ONLY by population: which claims a semantic change falsifies is not decidable short of running them, so the honest mechanism is to RUN the population a core-compiler change reaches, not to compute a narrower one.",

    "NEXT-RUNG TRIGGERS, NAMING THE CAPABILITY. Shape half: an Arrow is constructible only through the typed `v2.std.compilers.body_lowering` `declared_signature` constructor (which #12361 introduced as the single fixture mint), with a discriminating RED fixture that builds a named domain without the order edge and is REFUSED at construction, and a positive control through the constructor. Owed by its own change under the same ruling. Behaviour half: a GATE-POPULATION decision -- the `v2.test.*` claim population enrolled on the merge path for diffs that touch `src/v2/compiler` or `src/v2/std/node.dag` -- sufficient for EVERY claim that exercises a core stage to execute on such a diff, not for a selected subset. That is a job-roster and runner-budget decision reserved to the operator (DESIGN Building & checks: the job roster is closed to growth without sign-off), raised by neat-boar-16; until it is made, a green required floor on a core-compiler diff is evidence about the gate population ONLY, and a PR changing a core stage owes its own base-vs-head sweep of the claims it can reach, reported by identity.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v2.workflow.required_floor", decl_name: "required_gate_prefixes", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v2.workflow.required_floor", decl_name: "required_floor_disposition_with_changed_selection", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v2.workflow.floor_subject_seed", decl_name: "interface_reader_population", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v2.std.compilers.body_lowering", decl_name: "declared_signature", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v2.std.node", decl_name: "arrow_signature_order_conforms", field: WholeDeclaration },
  ],
}
